  "prediction": "**Summary of the Report: Implementation of CAPTA Requirements for Substance-Affected Infants**\n\nThe report examines the implementation of the Child Abuse Prevention and Treatment Act (CAPTA) requirements for substance-affected infants. CAPTA provides formula grants to states to improve child protective service systems, including policies and procedures for addressing substance-affected infants. The report highlights the challenges states face in implementing these requirements, particularly in the wake of the opioid epidemic.\n\n**Key Findings:**\n\n* 42 states require healthcare providers to notify Child Protective Services (CPS) about substance-affected infants, but some states lack clear policies and procedures.\n* 49 states reported that they have policies and procedures in place to ensure the safety and well-being of substance-affected infants, but many states struggle to implement these policies effectively.\n* States face challenges in defining \"substance-affected\" infants and determining which infants require notification to CPS.\n* Many states lack clear guidance on developing plans of safe care for substance-affected infants, which are required under CAPTA.\n* States report that they need additional guidance and technical assistance from the Administration for Children and Families (ACF) to implement CAPTA requirements effectively.\n\n**Recommendations:**\n\n* The Secretary of Health and Human Services should direct ACF to provide additional guidance and technical assistance to states to help them implement CAPTA requirements for substance-affected infants.\n* ACF should provide clear and consistent guidance on defining \"substance-affected\" infants and developing plans of safe care for these infants.\n\n**Conclusion:**\n\nThe report highlights the need for improved guidance and technical assistance from ACF to help states implement CAPTA requirements for substance-affected infants. Effective implementation of these requirements is critical to ensuring the safety and well-being of vulnerable infants and families affected by the opioid epidemic.",
